course: religion-ethics
lesson: 52
title: Getting old: how long people and animals live
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every living body slowly wears out as the years go by: skin gets thinner and wrinkly, bones get weaker, cuts heal more slowly, and doctors can help people stay well for longer but nobody can stop growing old.
explain: Picture Charlie's great-grandmother at her 100th birthday, a card from the King on the mantelpiece: once she was nine, skipping in a garden with her friends, and that child is still part of her.
explain: Each kind of living thing has its own usual length of life: a hamster about two or three years, a dog about ten to fifteen, many people seventy, eighty or more, some tortoises over a hundred, and a bristlecone pine tree nearly five thousand.
explain: The Bible says there is «a time to be born and a time to die»; Christians see old age as part of the life God gives, not a punishment, and every faith teaches respect for the old.
explain: Some old people get an illness of the brain called dementia that makes them forget, even the names of people they love; it is the illness forgetting, not the love going away.
fact: As people grow old, skin gets thinner, bones get weaker and the body heals more slowly; nobody can stop ageing.
fact: In countries like Britain, many people now live into their 70s, 80s or more; reaching 100 is rare, and the King sends a card to people in the UK who turn 100.
fact: Hamsters usually live about two or three years; dogs about ten to fifteen; cats often a little longer; some tortoises over a hundred years.
fact: Small animals usually have shorter lives than big ones, though there are exceptions.
fact: A short life, like a hamster's, can still be a happy one when the animal is well cared for every day.
fact: Dogs age faster than people: an old dog sleeps more, moves slowly and often gets grey fur round its muzzle.
fact: Some bristlecone pine trees in America are nearly 5,000 years old, while an adult mayfly may live only a day.
fact: Dementia is an illness of the brain that makes people forget and get confused; it is not a sign that they love people less.
fact: Many very old people live in care homes, where staff help them wash, eat and move; some are lonely, and visits matter a lot.
fact: People live longer where there is clean water, enough food, good doctors and peace; poverty and war shorten lives.
fact: Nobody knows exactly when a person will die, and many people live well into their 80s and 90s.
fact: Jesus taught that visiting someone in need is like visiting him; Christians and people of many faiths visit the old and lonely.
fact: The words «a time to be born and a time to die» come from the book of Ecclesiastes in the Bible.
fact: Many cultures, including Chinese and Ukrainian families, give the oldest people great honour.
exercise: Charlie's great-grandmother turns 100 and a card comes from the King. What is true about reaching 100? | It is rare and special; most people live to 70, 80 or more | Everyone in Britain reaches 100 nowadays | Nobody has ever lived past 100 | The King's card makes people live longer
exercise: Mia asks why Grandad's hands are wrinkly and he walks with a stick. Why do people get old? | The body slowly wears out over the years | Old people forget to drink water | Wrinkles come from staying in the bath too long | Walking sticks make the body weaker
exercise: Can doctors stop people growing old? | No, they can help people stay well longer, but not stop ageing | Yes, with one injection at the age of sixty, which lasts for ever | Yes, but only for people who eat no sugar | No, because doctors are not allowed to help old people
exercise: Finn is told his hamster will live «about two years, mate». Is that normal for a hamster? | Yes, hamsters usually live about two or three years | No, hamsters usually live just about as long as people do | No, the shop man was joking; they live fifty years | Yes, but only if it never leaves its cage
exercise: Lily's nana calls her by her mum's name. What is the most likely reason? | An illness like dementia makes her forget, not a lack of love | Nana has stopped loving Lily | Nana is playing a trick to tease her | Lily has changed her name without telling her
exercise: Tom's teacher shows a pine tree nearly 5,000 years old. What does it show? | Each living thing has its own natural length of life | Trees are more important than people | Trees never die at all | Everything alive lives about the same length of time in the end
exercise: At his 80th, Grandad jokes, «I'm not old, I'm vintage!» His grandson asks, «r u gonna die soon?» What is true? | Old people die one day, but nobody knows when | Everyone dies on exactly their 81st birthday, no sooner | Asking about death out loud always makes it happen | People who joke about getting old live for ever
exercise: Noah visits a care home and a lady holds his hand tight. Why might his visit matter? | Some people there are lonely, and a child's visit can light up their week | Care home rules say every child must visit once | The lady wanted to take his hand to keep it | Visits do not matter, because old people do not notice
exercise: Sienna sees a photo of her gran aged nine in a garden in Lviv. What does it show? | Every old person was once a child, and that child is still part of them | Her gran was a completely different person when she was young, not the same one | Photos from Lviv are always of famous people | Gran was never young; the photo is of someone else
exercise: Harry's old dog has a grey muzzle and sleeps all day. Why? | Dogs age faster than people, and old dogs sleep more | The dog is bored and pretending to be old | The dog has eaten far too much flour from the kitchen floor | Only dogs that are ill ever sleep
exercise: Zoe hears people in some countries live much longer. What helps people live longer? | Clean water, enough food, good doctors and peace | Living near the sea and eating fish only, every single day | Being born in summer rather than winter | Speaking English rather than another language
exercise: What does the Bible say about life and death in the book of Ecclesiastes? | There is a time to be born and a time to die | Only bad people ever grow old and die, not good ones | Old age is a punishment sent down from God for sins | People should try as hard as they can never to grow old
exercise: What did Jesus teach about visiting people in need? | Visiting someone in need is like visiting him | Only priests and nuns should ever visit the old | People in need should go and visit Jesus instead | Visiting the sick will always make you ill too
exercise: Which animal usually lives longest? | Some tortoises, over a hundred years | A hamster | An adult mayfly | A goldfish in a bowl
exercise: How do many Chinese and Ukrainian families treat their oldest members? | With great honour and respect | As people who no longer matter | As children who must obey the young | As guests who should live elsewhere
test: «100!!! how is she not dead yet» blurts Charlie. What would be a kinder way to ask about her long life? | «What was it like when you were small?» | «Are you going to die tomorrow, then, Gran?» | «Why are you still here?» | «Can I have your house when you go?»
test: A Gen Z cousin says, «Grandad walking with a stick is lowkey sad, can't the docs just fix ageing, no cap?» What is true? | Doctors help people stay well longer, but nobody can stop ageing | Yes, doctors can now reverse ageing completely with a simple pill | No, doctors never help old people at all | Yes, but only for rich grandads
test: Finn moans, «TWO YEARS? my shoes are older than that!» What is the wise reply? | A short life can still be happy, so make the time count | Then a hamster isn't worth looking after properly at all | Buy new shoes so the hamster seems older | Ask the shop for a hamster that lives for twenty years
test: Lily's friend goes, «your nan forgot your name, she's well sus, she doesn't love you.» («Sus» means «suspicious, dodgy».) What is the best answer? | Dementia makes her forget; the love is still there | Right, forgetting your name means all the love has gone | Right, so Lily should stop visiting her nan altogether | Nana is only pretending to forget, just to annoy her
test: What would happen if every living thing lived exactly the same number of years? | Nature would be very different, because each kind now has its own life span | Nothing would change at all | Every animal would be the size of a tree | Mayflies would become the oldest creatures
test: Noah's mate from Leeds says the care home was «right mardy and smelt funny». («Mardy» means «grumpy, moody».) What is the wise view? | Feeling awkward is normal, but the visit still matters a lot | He is right, so he should never go back there again, not ever | Care homes are only for people who smell | Old people do not notice who visits them
test: Sienna's little brother, from Lviv, says, «Babusia was never a kid, she was always old.» («Babusia» is Ukrainian for «granny».) Which reply is true? | Every old person was once a child like him | Grandmothers are born old and stay that way | Only grandads were once children, not grannies | People in Lviv are never children, they start grown
test: A Texan uncle says, «Y'all, that old dog's fixin' to sleep the whole day again.» («Fixin' to» means «about to».) Why is the dog sleeping so much? | Old dogs sleep more and slow down as they age | The dog is lazy and needs a telling-off | Old dogs sleep because they are bored of people | Sleeping makes dogs grow younger
test: A wise judge asks: «Why might people in some places die younger, and what could be done?» Best answer? | Poverty and war harm health; people can help with clean water, food and doctors | Some people are simply born weaker, and nothing can be done | Short lives are always the people's own fault | Long lives come only from luck, so helping is pointless
test: A cartoon shows a wizard drinking a potion and staying young for ever. What is the truth? | Every body ages, and no potion can stop it | Such potions exist but are very expensive | Wizards really do stay young for ever and ever | Only cartoon characters ever grow old
test: Grandad at his party says, «Not if I can help it, cheeky. I'm planning on seeing you grow up.» What does he mean? | He hopes to live a long time and is not upset by the question | He is angry and wants the child to leave | He knows exactly the day and the very hour when he is going to die | He is going to grow up again himself
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the process of human ageing? | A gradual deterioration of bodily functions which medicine may slow but cannot arrest | A process which may be entirely reversed by medical intervention | A phenomenon confined to persons who neglect their health | A punishment imposed upon those who have done wrong
exam: Identify the principal cause of forgetfulness in an elderly person diagnosed with dementia. | An illness affecting the brain, not any diminution of affection | A deliberate refusal to recall the names of relatives and friends | A lack of love for the members of the family | Excessive attendance at social occasions
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the life span of a domestic hamster. | It ordinarily extends to approximately two or three years | It ordinarily extends to approximately fifteen years | It ordinarily extends to approximately one hundred years or more | It ordinarily extends to approximately one day
exam: Ascertain the conditions principally associated with greater life expectancy within a population. | Clean water, adequate nutrition, medical care and peace | Residence in a cold climate exclusively, far from the sea | Membership of a particular sporting club | The avoidance of all physical exercise
